-- Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two, three military combat vehicles (printed 224-231): Badger, Centipede, Echelon Hovertank
-- 3 vehicle rows, with 52 locations and 12 weapon entries.
--
-- One-off data script, run once per environment. NOT a migration - it adds
-- rows, not schema.
--
--   node scripts/d1-apply.mjs --local apps/character-creator/db/~326-northern-gun-2-combat-vehicles-a.sql
--
-- Written by scripts/vessel-sql.mjs from 1 worker file(s). Every value
-- is printable ASCII, every row cites its book, and a Main Body location
-- agrees with its vehicle's mdc_main_body.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, is_mega_damage, ar, cost, cost_note, description, source_book) VALUES
  ('ng-bad202-badger', 'NG-BAD202 Badger Military Strike Vehicle', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Five: a pilot, a co-pilot, a communications specialist and two gunners, with room for up to five more crew or teammates.', 'The lower deck carries 8 power armor troops (by their bulk) or 14 infantry in comfort, 12 or 20 packed in.', '90 mph (144 km) maximum on pavement, dirt and gravel roads, stony desert, grassland, broken roads and rough ground; half that or less on sandy desert and in water or snow from 1 ft (0.3 m) to 10 ft (3 m) deep. Woodland and thick brush are likely impassable without a well-used path, and even then under 10 mph (16 km).', NULL, 'Drives through water up to 10 ft (3 m) deep.', 'Height 20 ft (6.1 m); width 13 ft (4 m); length 32 ft (9.8 m), or 40 ft (12.2 m) with the optional rear observation deck', '19 tons empty', 345, 1, NULL, 19000000, '22 million credits nuclear, 19 million Solid Oxide; 3.5 million less without the ion cannon. The optional observation deck is 230,000 credits extra.', 'Model NG-BAD202 (Class: All-Terrain Armored Personnel Carrier and Exploration Vehicle), nicknamed the Big Bad. Picture an oversized, armoured Big Boss ATV or a small Mountaineer: part ATV, part APC and part tank, from the Northern Gun team of Ron Guest, Jacob Healy, Charlie Hughie and Alexander Heck, on sale since spring 109 P.A. It moves infantry or power armor squads and cargo, patrols borders, scouts far afield and explores. A rail gun and a lidded mini-missile box sit side by side in the nose, a big ion cannon turret rides on top with its own gunner, and the sensor and communications gear stands on the left beside the armoured cockpit. Inside are two decks. The lower one is an open cargo bay whose bench seats rise out of the floor for an adventuring party or a squad of 8-14 troops; the upper one, only 5.5 ft (1.7 m) high, holds ten enclosed, fairly quiet sleeping berths stacked in two columns of five, each with a light, a monitor and a small computer, and a walkway circling past ten long window slits. Combat plexiglass fills the slits and heavy plates can slide over them, or the glass can be lowered so the slits become gun ports, each with a fold-down chair. Crazies, Headhunters and Combat Cyborgs are oddly fond of it. Buyers may skip the turret to save money, add a rear open-air observation platform 7 ft (2.1 m) across with a 2.5 ft (0.76 m) rail (room for 3-4 people, two heavy or three light to medium weapons, or 5 tons of cargo), or give up the lower bay for living space or labs at extra cost. The pilot sits low in the nose with the co-pilot, communications officer and gunners above and behind; the standard combat model has no windows there at all, only armour and a reinforced inner compartment, and almost no buyer takes the plexiglass option. Losing a wheel cuts speed 35% and costs -25% driving, and riding on the rim can do more harm; losing two strands it until they are replaced. With the Main Body gone it shuts down and is useless. Penalties: none on pavement, grass, dirt roads, gravel, stony desert and broken roads; -5% piloting on sand and gravel; -10% in mud to 2 ft (0.6 m); -15% on ice and in water or snow 5-10 ft (1.5-3 m) deep. It crosses dunes and shallow water, but forest and dense growth are likely impassable. Range: unlimited. Cargo: an 8 x 5 x 9 ft (2.4 x 1.5 x 2.7 m) bay, a little space in each berth, a backpack, canteen and rifle behind each cockpit seat, and up to 5 tons on the observation deck. Power: Solid Oxide or nuclear, 10 year life. Communications and sensors: as giant combat robots, plus a rear long-range array with a relay dish good for 200 miles (320 km), a third of that in mountains, underground or in storms; scrambled audio and video; enough to act as a field relay station, passing data on to other Badgers, Bigfoot robots, command bases or towers to build a network hundreds or thousands of miles long. Its enhanced radar tracks 120 targets out to 100 miles (160 km) with a friend-or-foe library that ranks threats, and gives the Badger and any robot, tank or power armor taking its feed within one mile (1.6 km) +1 on initiative and +1 to strike. Cameras: two concealed in the nose and one in the spotlight (48 hours of footage), plus advanced cameras in the turret''s independent sensor array and two in the left-side sensor sphere, a ball turret turning 360 degrees and tilting 180 (10x telescopic to 2 miles/3.2 km, 12x macro, 96 hours of video and 8,000 stills; the sphere''s has passive nightvision). The left-side pillar carries a halogen and an infrared spotlight, each moving 20 degrees in its housing, the housing turning 360 degrees and tilting 45. Only the cockpit and crew compartment are sealed and environmental; the cargo bay and the upper gunners'' deck are open.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.224-227'),
  ('ng-rsv7a-centipede', 'NG-RSV7A Centipede Robot Transport and Recovery Vehicle', 'rifts', 'vehicle', '13-24. A skeleton crew of 13: seven in the front locomotive (pilot, co-pilot, communications specialist, gunner, the head''s Repair and Recovery unit pilot and two Operators) and six in the rear (two crane Operators, a pilot, a co-pilot and two assistant Operators). Often 4-8 more Operators for robot mechanics and weapons, and 2-6 defenders, usually in power armor; sometimes 1-2 cargo hauler robots and 4-12 laborers.', '16 in the passenger area over the cargo bay (24 cramped); up to three in the front crew compartment; the rear section''s quarters sleep 18. On runs within 100 miles (160 km) of base the crew may double, riding in the cargo bay and on the flatbed.', '60 mph (96 km) maximum empty or half loaded; 45 mph (72 km) with more than 135 tons aboard; 30 mph (48 km) or less over very rugged ground and ice. It manages sand, gravel and snow to 9 ft (2.7 m), but woodland and mud over 2 ft (0.6 m) deep must be gone around. Disconnected from the flatbed, each locomotive section can reach 65 mph (104 km).', NULL, 'Fords water up to 9 ft (2.7 m) deep.', 'Height 41 ft (12.5 m), the crane reaching 80 ft (24.4 m); width 36 ft (11 m); length 174 ft (53 m) overall: front section 40 ft (12.2 m), its head extending or rising a further 14 ft (4.3 m), flatbed 90 ft (27.4 m), rear section 44 ft (13.4 m)', '158 tons empty: front section 52, flatbed and treads 42, rear section and crane 64', 500, 1, NULL, 85000000, '92 million credits nuclear or 85 million Solid Oxide for the whole three-section vehicle, a full missile load included; reloads extra.', 'Model NG-RSV7A (Class: Robot Support Vehicle). A huge three-section machine for moving, repairing, supplying and salvaging giant robots and military vehicles, sold to nations, armies, mercenary companies and robot dealers since 106 P.A., though few have heard of it until lately; the design team was led by Patrick LeHoullier, Jeff A. Lee, Russell Lenhares, G.J. Ullman and Lano Twescharl. It brings fresh ''Bots to the front and hauls disabled robots and armour off it. Its guns are for protecting cargo worth tens of millions, not for fighting. A front tractor locomotive pulls while a rear tractor with a heavy crane pushes, with a flatbed on two great tread sets between them carrying 2-3 giant robots, big vehicles or heavy equipment, held by railings, straps and locks. The front section carries the plasma cannon and missile batteries and a fearsome insect-like head that is no weapon: a partly automated repair and recovery unit whose four mandibles clamp a damaged robot while a robot arm, cutting lasers, torches, drills and saws mend it or carefully strip off limbs, weapons and the pilot compartment, rescuing any crew still inside. The head tilts 45 degrees, turns 30 to each side and reaches 14 ft (4.3 m) further out or up, and the two turret-like housings flanking it are heavy cutting and welding lasers. In support work it swaps limbs and weapons, reloads rail guns and launchers, replaces sensors, patches armour (salvaged plates included) and refills hydraulic fluid, coolant, water, ammunition drums and napalm, 25-50% faster than an ordinary ground crew or garage. Under the head a large sliding blast door opens on a cavernous bay for loose parts; above it is the passenger area with a refrigerator, a hot and cold sink and three tables. Pilot, gunner, communications specialist and up to three others ride in a crew compartment on the head''s right, with reclining seats, a small refrigerator and a supply closet. The rear section houses the crane crew and 18 in two-person rooms, with a kitchenette, a dining and meeting room, a storeroom and a washroom (three sinks, four toilets, four urinals, two showers). Each section has its own main body, and this row''s mdc_main_body is the front section''s 500; the head has 350, the flatbed 350 and the rear section 400. Losing the head ends all repair work, and the whole head must be replaced, but the front section still drives and fights. Losing the front section kills its weapons, head, main sensors and drive; the rear locomotive can drag it home at 20 mph (32 km) with the bed and bay empty, 8 mph (13 km) with any real load, or 20 mph if it is cut loose. Losing the flatbed freezes its treads, so both locomotives together drag it at 15 mph (24 km); 2D4x10% of its load falls off, each big item or crate taking 1D6x10 M.D.C., nothing fallen can be reloaded, and every 20 miles (32 km) of travel the rest has a 01-50% chance to fall too. The two locomotives can uncouple and leave at up to 65 mph (104 km), some staying to guard costly cargo unless attack continues. Losing the rear section kills the crane and its drive; the front locomotive drags it as the rear one would drag the front. Strength: the head is Robot P.S. 50, the crane Robot P.S. 60. Cargo: 252 tons overall. The forward bay holds 50 tons in 25 x 12 ft (7.6 x 3.7 m) with a 16 ft (4.9 m) ceiling; the head lifts 38 tons; the flatbed holds 164 tons on 90 x 32 ft (27.4 x 9.8 m); the crane lifts 60 tons but carries only 30 above 15 mph (24 km); the rear section has a 6 x 6 x 4 ft (1.8 x 1.8 x 1.2 m) supply space and 120 gallon (454 liter) tanks of hydraulic fluid/coolant and of water. Uncoupled, the front section pulls 94 tons and the rear 130. Power: Solid Oxide or nuclear only, about 12 years on nuclear. Sensors: as giant combat robots, plus a 2,000 ft (610 m) thermal imager, infrared and ultraviolet optics, and scrambled wideband radio and video good for 200 miles (320 km), a third of that in mountains, underground or in storms. Dashboard cameras (48 hours) sit in the crew compartment nose, each spotlight and its back facing the flatbed, with four more on the flatbed and two on the crane; advanced cameras (10x telescopic to 2 miles/3.2 km, 12x macro, 96 hours of video and 8,000 stills) sit in the main sensor array, which adds passive nightvision, in the head''s nose (two) and in the robot arm. The head cockpit, crew compartment, cargo bay, passenger area and living quarters are sealed environmental spaces.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.227-230'),
  ('ng-eht27-echelon-hovertank', 'NG-EHT27 Echelon Hovertank', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Three: a driver, a communications engineer and a gunner/commander.', 'One.', 'Hover only: 110 mph (176 km) maximum.', 'Cannot fly; rises no more than 20 ft (6.1 m) off the ground.', 'None.', 'Height 13 ft (4 m); width 9 ft (2.7 m); length 28 ft (8.5 m)', '16 tons with a full weapon load', 350, 1, NULL, 12000000, '16 million credits nuclear (12 year life), 12 million Solid Oxide.', 'Model NG-EHT27 (Class: Hovertank). Northern Gun''s answer to the tanks and armoured vehicles of Iron Heart Armaments, and an unusual one: in place of easily damaged treads it floats on a big, experimental jet hover system, so without its gun it would be closer to a heavy hover truck. Every NG tank counts as experimental, which its buyers among nations, mercenary companies, militias and adventurers seem not to mind. Speed lets it answer raids fast and shift position in battle, and it suits killing giant robots, tanks and big monsters, which draws monster-hunting mercenaries and small kingdoms troubled by dinosaurs. The main gun is a further experiment: a rail gun barrel over a heavy ion cannon barrel, with a grenade launcher on the roof beneath them, and computerised fire control lets the crew use every weapon from inside. Its best protection is its speed, low profile, size and agility. Each hover jet or thruster lost costs 15% of speed and a cumulative -5% driving. With the Main Body gone it shuts down and is useless. Hover system: the undercarriage jets hold it up, while the four large hover thrusters and the two rear jets give speed and sharp turns and stops; each large thruster swivels 70 degrees up and down and 20 to the sides. Cargo: a weapons locker in the crew compartment holds 4-6 rifles and pistols with two spare E-Clips apiece, three NG survival packs, four 2 gallon (7.6 liter) water cans, four walkie-talkies, a field radio and four backpacks. Power: nuclear (about 12 years) or Solid Oxide. Sensors: standard for environmental vehicles, as giant combat robots.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.230-231');

